Diastereoisomers have different physical properties, so they can have different NMR chemical shifts and different LC-MS retention times. They would have same mass. So mixture of diastereoisomers fits.

But wait, the compound is a small peptide. Peptides are chiral, and if synthesized, could have epimerization at the alpha-carbon during coupling, leading to a mixture of diastereoisomers (e.g., L/D amino acid). This is a common problem in peptide synthesis: racemization/epimerization at the C-terminal residue during coupling, giving a mixture of diastereoisomers. These diastereoisomers have same mass, different retention times, and different NMR shifts for the alpha proton.

