We need answer. Need solve. Problem: pK_a' values for aquatic mammals HHbO2 and HHb are 6.62 and 8.18 respectively. Calculate number moles H+ that hemoglobin binds when releasing one mole oxygen at pH 7.4.

This is classic Haldane effect: oxygenation changes pK. For deoxy Hb (HHb) pK=8.18, oxy Hb (HHbO2) pK=6.62. At pH 7.4, protonation state changes. Need calculate H+ released/absorbed? "binds when releasing one mole oxygen" means deoxygenation: HHbO2 -> HHb + O2. At pH 7.4, compare protonation of oxy vs deoxy. pK values: For HHbO2 pK=6.62, for HHb pK=8.18. At pH 7.4, oxy pK < pH so mostly deprotonated? Need Henderson-Hasselbalch: protonated form fraction = [HHb]/total? For acid HA <-> H+ + A-. pK. At pH > pK, more A- (deprotonated). For oxy pK 6.62, pH 7.4: pH-pK=0.78, ratio A-/HA=10^0.78=6.03, protonated fraction =1/(1+6.03)=0.142. For deoxy pK 8.18, pH < pK by 0.78, ratio A-/HA=10^-0.78=0.166, protonated fraction =1/(1+0.166)=0.857. So deoxy binds more H+ (protonated) than oxy. When releasing O2 (oxy -> deoxy), hemoglobin binds H+ amount = fraction protonated deoxy - fraction protonated oxy = 0.857 - 0.142 = 0.715. So ~0.716 H+/O2. Choice F.

